      "text": "The Scalar-Angular-Torsion (SAT) framework is fundamentally a conservative reparameterization of established physics rather than a radical departure from it\n1\n2\n. Instead of introducing a new ontological framework or fundamentally new mathematical objects, it takes the standard particle worldline used in Minkowski spacetime and makes a different modeling choice about how much structural information that worldline is allowed to carry\n1\n3\n. While conventional physics attaches properties like mass, charge, and spin through external fields and internal symmetries, SAT reassigns those internal degrees of freedom to the geometric structure along the worldline itself\n1\n4\n.\nThe approach is technically orthodox because it remains grounded in a standard variational framework where you define a Lagrangian—representing the sum of kinetic, interaction, and potential energies—and derive equations of motion from it\n5\n6\n. It treats the Standard Model and General Relativity as essentially correct, \"importing\" them into a geometric grammar where their axioms are reinterpreted as mandatory structural invariants\n7\nmore_horiz\n. This means the framework is designed to reduce cleanly to known formalisms in appropriate limits, acting as an extension of existing physics rather than a replacement\n3\n11\n.\nUnification is achieved by demonstrating a Relativistic-Quantum Isomorphism, which suggests that General Relativity and Quantum Mechanics are simply two projection regimes of the same underlying dynamics\n12\n13\n. In this view, General Relativity governs the global, low-frequency bending energy of worldlines as they resist the radial expansion of the manifold, while Quantum Mechanics governs the discrete, high-frequency vibrational modes of those same worldlines\n12\nmore_horiz\n. This allows the curvature of a relativistic geodesic to be mathematically identical to the stiffness of a quantum mode, resolved as different rotation planes of the same spheric